{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And Jacob called, etc., which, etc., Beth El.</b> It is difficult: did he not already call it thus, and with the addition of ״El Beth El,״ as I explained above (Genesis 35:7)? What does he innovate in this naming? Perhaps it may be that he added to call the place adjacent to Beth El — that it too should be called ״Beth El״ because HaShem spoke to him there. This is what the verse precisely says: <b>the name of the place where [He] spoke</b>, etc. — meaning that on account of the speech that occurred there he called it as well, etc., for if it referred to the place where He had appeared to him when he fled, it would not have needed to say ״where [He] spoke.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:09.071773992Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}